                <pb facs="0190" n="66" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg66"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A4002/4: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harvey</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-09-03T12:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 3,
                                1921—noon</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>523. Your 721 of September 2. Will assume that there will be four delegates.
                    Would it be possible, in Curzon’s absence, to obtain authoritative suggestions
                    regarding agenda? The Japanese Government expects that the question will be
                    considered shortly as they desire definitely to know the scope of the agenda by
                    September 15, as their delegation must leave by October 1. I had intended soon
                    to make informal suggestions and desire, if practicable, to be able to obtain
                    British Government’s views.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
